@anon.6e01a69b2c234dfdc5e23dbc943ea0bc.1273 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [6207 x i8] }> <{ [6207 x i8] c"A color gradient.\0A\0ATypst supports linear gradients through the\0A[`gradient.linear` function]($gradient.linear), radial gradients through\0Athe [`gradient.radial` function]($gradient.radial), and conic gradients\0Athrough the [`gradient.conic` function]($gradient.conic).\0A\0AA gradient can be used for the following purposes:\0A- As a fill to paint the interior of a shape:\0A  `{rect(fill: gradient.linear(..))}`\0A- As a stroke to paint the outline of a shape:\0A  `{rect(stroke: 1pt + gradient.linear(..))}`\0A- As the fill of text:\0A  `{set text(fill: gradient.linear(..))}`\0A- As a color map you can [sample]($gradient.sample) from:\0A  `{gradient.linear(..).sample(0.5)}`\0A\0A# Examples\0A```example\0A>>> #set square(size: 50pt)\0A#stack(\0A  dir: ltr,\0A  spacing: 1fr,\0A  square(fill: gradient.linear(..color.map.rainbow)),\0A  square(fill: gradient.radial(..color.map.rainbow)),\0A  square(fill: gradient.conic(..color.map.rainbow)),\0A)\0A```\0A\0AGradients are also supported on text, but only when setting the\0A[relativeness]($gradient.relative) to either `{auto}` (the default value) or\0A`{\22parent\22}`. To create word-by-word or glyph-by-glyph gradients, you can\0Awrap the words or characters of your text in [boxes]($box) manually or\0Athrough a [show rule]($styling/#show-rules).\0A\0A```example\0A>>> #set page(width: auto, height: auto, margin: 12pt)\0A>>> #set text(size: 12pt)\0A#set text(fill: gradient.linear(red, blue))\0A#let rainbow(content) = {\0A  set text(fill: gradient.linear(..color.map.rainbow))\0A  box(content)\0A}\0A\0AThis is a gradient on text, but with a #rainbow[twist]!\0A```\0A\0A# Stops\0AA gradient is composed of a series of stops. Each of these stops has a color\0Aand an offset. The offset is a [ratio]($ratio) between `{0%}` and `{100%}` or\0Aan angle between `{0deg}` and `{360deg}`. The offset is a relative position\0Athat determines how far along the gradient the stop is located. The stop's\0Acolor is the color of the gradient at that position. You can choose to omit\0Athe offsets when defining a gradient. In this case, Typst will space all\0Astops evenly.\0A\0A# Relativeness\0AThe location of the `{0%}` and `{100%}` stops depends on the dimensions\0Aof a container. This container can either be the shape that it is being\0Apainted on, or the closest surrounding container. This is controlled by the\0A`relative` argument of a gradient constructor. By default, gradients are\0Arelative to the shape they are being painted on, unless the gradient is\0Aapplied on text, in which case they are relative to the closest ancestor\0Acontainer.\0A\0ATypst determines the ancestor container as follows:\0A- For shapes that are placed at the root/top level of the document, the\0A  closest ancestor is the page itself.\0A- For other shapes, the ancestor is the innermost [`block`] or [`box`] that\0A  contains the shape. This includes the boxes and blocks that are implicitly\0A  created by show rules and elements. For example, a [`rotate`] will not\0A  affect the parent of a gradient, but a [`grid`] will.\0A\0A# Color spaces and interpolation\0AGradients can be interpolated in any color space. By default, gradients are\0Ainterpolated in the [Oklab]($color.oklab) color space, which is a\0A[perceptually uniform](https://programmingdesignsystems.com/color/perceptually-uniform-color-spaces/index.html)\0Acolor space. This means that the gradient will be perceived as having a\0Asmooth progression of colors. This is particularly useful for data\0Avisualization.\0A\0AHowever, you can choose to interpolate the gradient in any supported color\0Aspace you want, but beware that some color spaces are not suitable for\0Aperceptually interpolating between colors. Consult the table below when\0Achoosing an interpolation space.\0A\0A|           Color space           | Perceptually uniform? |\0A| ------------------------------- |-----------------------|\0A| [Oklab]($color.oklab)           | *Yes*                 |\0A| [Oklch]($color.oklch)           | *Yes*                 |\0A| [sRGB]($color.rgb)              | *No*                  |\0A| [linear-RGB]($color.linear-rgb) | *Yes*                 |\0A| [CMYK]($color.cmyk)             | *No*                  |\0A| [Grayscale]($color.luma)        | *Yes*                 |\0A| [HSL]($color.hsl)               | *No*                  |\0A| [HSV]($color.hsv)               | *No*                  |\0A\0A```preview\0A>>> #set text(fill: white, font: \22IBM Plex Sans\22, 8pt)\0A>>> #set block(spacing: 0pt)\0A#let spaces = (\0A  (\22Oklab\22, color.oklab),\0A  (\22Oklch\22, color.oklch),\0A  (\22linear-RGB\22, color.linear-rgb),\0A  (\22sRGB\22, color.rgb),\0A  (\22CMYK\22, color.cmyk),\0A  (\22HSL\22, color.hsl),\0A  (\22HSV\22, color.hsv),\0A  (\22Grayscale\22, color.luma),\0A)\0A\0A#for (name, space) in spaces {\0A  block(\0A    width: 100%,\0A    inset: 4pt,\0A    fill: gradient.linear(\0A      red,\0A      blue,\0A      space: space,\0A    ),\0A    strong(upper(name)),\0A  )\0A}\0A```\0A\0A# Direction\0ASome gradients are sensitive to direction. For example, a linear gradient\0Ahas an angle that determines its direction. Typst uses a clockwise angle,\0Awith 0\C2\B0 being from left to right, 90\C2\B0 from top to bottom, 180\C2\B0 from right to\0Aleft, and 270\C2\B0 from bottom to top.\0A\0A```example\0A>>> #set square(size: 50pt)\0A#stack(\0A  dir: ltr,\0A  spacing: 1fr,\0A  square(fill: gradient.linear(red, blue, angle: 0deg)),\0A  square(fill: gradient.linear(red, blue, angle: 90deg)),\0A  square(fill: gradient.linear(red, blue, angle: 180deg)),\0A  square(fill: gradient.linear(red, blue, angle: 270deg)),\0A)\0A```\0A\0A# Presets\0ATypst predefines color maps that you can use with your gradients. See the\0A[`color`]($color/#predefined-color-maps) documentation for more details.\0A\0A# Note on file sizes\0A\0AGradients can be quite large, especially if they have many stops. This is\0Abecause gradients are stored as a list of colors and offsets, which can\0Atake up a lot of space. If you are concerned about file sizes, you should\0Aconsider the following:\0A- SVG gradients are currently inefficiently encoded. This will be improved\0A  in the future.\0A- PDF gradients in the [`color.hsv`]($color.hsv), [`color.hsl`]($color.hsl),\0A  and [`color.oklch`]($color.oklch) color spaces are stored as a list of\0A  [`color.oklab`]($color.oklab) colors with extra stops in between. This\0A  avoids needing to encode these color spaces in your PDF file, but it does\0A  add extra stops to your gradient, which can increase the file size." }>, align 1
